  "summary": "Preprints audit foundation-model perturbation predictors against baselines and plate confounds, alongside context-aware and memory-based alternatives.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is single-cell perturbation response prediction and when its outputs can be trusted. Diagnostic work targets several failure sources. An audit of the STATE state-transition decoder reports that a low-rank attention bottleneck bounds reliability. A benchmark of five single-cell foundation models against simple baselines for annotation, in silico perturbation and GRN inference asks where foundation models actually help. A screen analysis shows that plate layout and well effects confound donor variation and benchmark evaluation, and it proposes design-aware estimators. Method papers restructure how context and response are represented. UnDetect separates cellular context from compound effects and fuses structure, targets, mechanisms and Cell Painting morphology to predict responses to unseen compounds. scDisentangle generates identity-preserving counterfactuals through sequential disentanglement. BioMeme replaces a learned state-to-response layer with a memory of measured experiments. CellCanon frames prediction, explanation and updating as an agentic world model. What is new is that reliability audits now appear alongside architectures that rely on explicit context or on measured data.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
